    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This work is concerned with the mathematical modeling of nonisothermal water injection using analytical techniques. Generalized procedures to interpret pressure transient injection and falloff data following the injection of cold water into a hot oil reservoir are presented.In many reservoirs water injection is initiated during the early stages of reservoir development. Typically, the injected water is significantly colder than the reservoir fluids. This, together with the relative permeability characteristics of oil and water, causes a saturation gradient to be established soon after injection begins. In analyzing the pressure transient data from injection/falloff tests these regions of different temperature and saturation may resemble permeability boundaries and care in the interpretation of the data is required. This work presents new analytical solutions to both the injection and the falloff problem. The solutions incorporate the effects of temperature and saturation gradients and give the pressure, saturations and temperature as functions of distance and time in a one dimensional radial reservoir. It is demonstrated that the saturation and temperature gradients have significant effects on the pressure data for both the injection and falloff periods.A two-step procedure is used to derive the solutions to the moving boundary injection problem. Assuming the fluids to be incompressible, we obtain a non-strictly hyperbolic system for saturation and temperature (Riemann problem). This problem is solved by using the method of characteristics together with the appropriate shock-admissibility criteria. With the knowledge of the saturation and temperature distributions, and hence the mobilities and diffusivities, from step one, the second order diffusivity equation is solved assuming slightly compressible fluids in step two.Both a similarity and a quasi-stationary method is used to solve the resulting moving boundary problem. The falloff solution is derived both by solving the complete initial-boundary value problem with the injection solution as the initial condition and by superposing solutions to the linear stationary variable mobility and diffusivity problem. It is demonstrated that the analytical solutions can be used to estimate fluid and reservoir properties. By using nonlinear regression techniques we can match field data and estimate important parameters such as absolute and relative permeabilities, heat capacities, distance to temperature and phase fronts as well as storage and skin coefficients.",
